.TH GIT-STAGE-BATCH-REWRITE 1 "August 2026" "git-stage-batch @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-stage-batch-rewrite \- execute deterministic history refinements
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-stage-batch rewrite scan
[\fB\-\-output\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
[\fIBOUNDARY\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite validate
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
\fIPLAN\fR
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite apply
[\fB\-\-allow-published-ref\fR \fIREF\fR]...
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
\fIPLAN\fR
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite status
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite continue
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite abort
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite verify
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rewrite scan
captures an immutable, linear commit range and emits a semantic KEEP plan
template. The snapshot contains full commit, parent, and tree object IDs;
exact author, committer, message, encoding, and signature state; and stable
patch-unit identities derived from streamed zero-context diffs.
.PP
Patch bytes are not copied into JSON. Each commit records its exact old and
new tree pair, from which later commands regenerate full-index binary patches
through bounded storage.
.PP
.B rewrite validate
strictly parses a reviewed scan document, regenerates every immutable fact
from live Git objects, and accepts KEEP, REWORD, and mechanically proven
INTEGRATE outputs. It requires exact source and patch-unit conservation,
original target authorship, complete replay, and the frozen final tree.
.PP
.B rewrite apply
creates an operation-owned recovery ref, builds a deterministic unsigned
replacement chain without changing the worktree, verifies it, then updates
the checked-out branch once with compare-and-swap. Commit objects preserve
the planned author, the target source committer, declared encoding, and exact
message bytes. Source signature headers are omitted and recorded by digest.
.PP
Apply does not contact a remote and never performs or authorizes a push or
force-push.
.PP
.B rewrite status
reports the closed phase, exact next action, output progress, recovery ref,
and independently rechecked resume preconditions for a durable operation.
Before the apply executor creates an operation, status reports that no
operation is active. After completion or abort, it reports the latest terminal
operation while leaving the active slot available for another plan.
.PP
.B rewrite continue
revalidates an active checkpoint and executes only its exact next action.
Pending output publication and final branch publication are reconciled
idempotently after interruption.
.PP
.B rewrite abort
records restore intent, retracts a pending output ref, and restores the
original branch only when the live tip is an exact value owned by the
operation. Foreign movement is never overwritten.
.PP
.B rewrite verify
independently reconstructs the active or latest complete output, rechecks all
commit metadata and topology, and compares its deterministic audit record to
the checkpoint digest.
.SH SCAN OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FILE"
Atomically write the reusable JSON document to
.IR FILE .
Existing regular-file permissions are preserved; symbolic-link destinations
are refused.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Write the complete reusable JSON document to standard output. This can be
combined with
.B \-\-output
when both a file and standard output are useful.
.TP
.I BOUNDARY
Commit excluded from the source range. By default, use the fork point or
merge base between HEAD and its configured upstream.
.SH VALIDATE OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON validation report with commit/unit counts, final tree,
signature count, and freshly collected mutation preconditions.
.SH STATUS OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print versioned JSON containing the complete durable checkpoint inspection.
.SH APPLY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-allow-published-ref " REF"
Permit local rewriting when a source commit is contained by the exact full
remote-tracking ref
.IR REF .
Repeat for every containing ref. This option does not authorize a push or
force-push.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON operation result.
.SH CONTINUE, ABORT, AND VERIFY OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON operation or verification result.
.SH PLAN CONTRACT
Only the
.B plan.outputs
array is semantic input. The
.B snapshot
object is immutable and is compared exactly after regeneration. The
.B safety
object is informational and is always recollected when a plan is validated.
.PP
Every output contains an operation, one or more source commit IDs, the complete
ordered source-unit list, a message and its declared encoding, the exact target
author, and an optional human rationale. Rationale prose is not treated as
mechanical proof.
.PP
KEEP requires the original message. REWORD permits a new message but preserves
the exact source patch, order, and author. INTEGRATE consumes at least two
source commits into the earliest target position. Intervening outputs retain
source order, every source patch is replayed exactly once through Git's real
three-way machinery, and the complete result must reproduce the frozen final
tree. Empty source commits remain explicit unless deliberately consumed by an
integration.
.SH SAFETY
Scan and validation do not change commits or refs. Validation materializes
candidate trees only in a temporary object quarantine. Both commands may
run while local mutation preconditions are blocked and label those blockers in
the safety report. They reject merges, stale or forged snapshots, duplicate
JSON fields, abbreviated object IDs, omitted or reordered units, and active
replace objects or legacy grafts. Scan compares the index directly to the
frozen final tree and does not invoke
.BR git-write-tree (1)
or create Git objects.
.PP
The safety report records tracked index/worktree cleanliness, staging-session
and saved-batch state, active Git/rewrite operations, upstream divergence,
remote refs containing every source commit, and untracked-path count. A remote
ref containing any selected commit blocks mutation unless a later apply
invocation records that exact verified review-head exception. Untracked paths
are reported but do not by themselves block an object-based rewrite whose
final tree is unchanged.
.PP
Source cryptographic signature headers are identified by header name and
content digest. Their payloads are not copied into the plan. Rewritten commit
objects cannot retain the validity of source signatures, which is reported
explicitly.
.PP
Durable operation checkpoints live below
.BR .git/git-stage-batch/rewrite/ .
They use private atomically replaced files, a closed phase/next-action schema,
and operation-owned recovery/output refs. A pending commit/tree checkpoint
closes the crash window around each output-ref update. A deterministic
verification record binds final tree, output topology, plan digest, and removed
signature digests. Status verifies the branch, expected tip, index, worktree,
plan and verification digests, output objects, recovery ref, and the output ref
that keeps a paused replacement chain reachable before declaring resume ready.
Manual recovery output uses compare-and-swap
.B git update-ref
syntax so unrelated ref movement is not overwritten.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-stage-batch (1),
.BR git-stage-batch-fixup (1),
.BR git-commit (1),
.BR git-rev-list (1),
.BR git-update-ref (1)
